WEBVTT

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.670
ERIKA HEIDI: Hi,
I'm Erika Heidi,

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.280
Staff DevRel Engineer
at Chainguard.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.000
In this short presentation,
I will share five strategies

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.680
to mitigate software supply
chain risks in GitHub actions.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.490
So the first thing you should
do is inspect your repositories

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.910
for insecure defaults
and other bad practices.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.220
For instance, using
pull request targets

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.290
with head code execution,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.870
or using long-lived
personal access tokens

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.230
with broad privileges,
direct shell execution

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.410
that can be manipulated
by external actors,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.080
and also actions pinned by tag.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.770
These are some things
that you should look for.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.000
I will talk a little bit more
about each of these things.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.400
Another thing is
unprotected main branch

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.310
and unprotected release tags.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.930
It can be a little
bit overwhelming,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.620
but the good news is that
you can use Copilot agent

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.980
in your repository
directly and ask it

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.400
for evaluate your GitHub actions

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.830
and find any potential
vulnerabilities.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.190
That was the prompt I
used, for instance, here.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.150
And it was very effective
in detecting the issues.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.020
So this was a repo that
I set up for testing.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:22.430
And I created intentionally a
lot of insecure things as a demo

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:26.120
to demonstrate
secret exfiltration.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.120
And so, this has a vulnerable
GitHub action workflow.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.500
And Copilot was very successful
in detecting the issues.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.380
Pull request targets
with code execution.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.970
So why that's an issue?

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:45.060
Because when someone creates
a pull request, then the code

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.010
from the pull
request is executed.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:51.480
And when you use pull request
targets, this is executed

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.480
in the context of the actual
main branch and all the secrets

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.330
and all the environment
variables that are used

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:05.190
in the actual builds
in the main branch.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.250
So any variables that you have,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.790
any secrets that you
have can be exfiltrated,

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:16.020
depending on how you run
your code in this workflow.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.860
And the Copilot detected
the pull request targets

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.900
with execution that
runs go setup.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.650
So this is a method -- this is
the same method that was used

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.730
for the initial
Trivy exploitation.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.580
So they exploited the
pull request targets

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:42.660
that was using PAT
with broad privileges.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.220
And another thing
was secrets exposed

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.920
to attack a controller binary.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.570
It's also critical.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.770
Write-capable token
also give permission

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.060
for writing the pull request.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.660
It's also high risk.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:07.560
And also, it also pointed out
the actions pinned to a tag.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.550
So this is insecure because
tags can be rewritten.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.030
And this was also used
in the Trivy attack.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:20.940
The attackers were able
to rewrite previous tags,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.090
all two points to the
same malicious commit.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.270
So if you were pinning
your action by tag,

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.940
you would pull the new codes

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.400
with malicious
injected payloads.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.270
So yes, this workflow is
definitely vulnerable.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.880
Ask Copilot to check
your repo and see

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.150
if it can find any
vulnerabilities.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:49.080
So you should protect your
branches, your main branch,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.630
especially in your tags so
they cannot be rewritten.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:58.950
This would prevent the
malicious code to be released

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.450
to other users and it
would contain propagation.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.550
And also, you should
protect your main branch

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:09.420
to prevent purge directly
to the main branch.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.020
And attackers can
also use stolen PAT

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.080
to inject directly
on the main branch.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.300
So this is also very important.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.820
And it's a setting that
you need to change.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.850
So by default, you don't
have this protection.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.250
So you need to go there
in your repo settings

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:33.000
and enable these protections.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.290
So the second tip important

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:42.180
for minimizing your risk is
minimizing your attack surface.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.840
So this is like one of
the simplest practices.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.840
In theory, it's so simple.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.650
Just make sure you have
a smaller attack surface

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:56.280
so you have less entry
points to be exploited.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.810
It's like a low-hanging fruit
that you cannot ignore.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.200
You have to think about direct
and transitive dependencies.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.540
So the dependencies of your
dependencies are also a risk.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.640
Any weak link in the
chain can be used

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.210
as a foothold to obtain access.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:21.060
And even with low-privileged
access, they can also try

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:26.100
to exfiltrate privileges and
some other type of exploitation.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.390
But once they get access, yeah,
there's a lot they can do.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:34.890
So the main idea is not
to remove functionality.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:39.300
So you don't want to remove
things that you actually use.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.970
But to remove what you don't
need, what doesn't need

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:45.210
to be there in your
runtime especially.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.280
Because there are
lots of dependencies

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.370
in the runtime level,

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:54.330
in the base OS level
that you don't need.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.670
And one way to do that is by
using minimal container images.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.060
So Chainguard containers
are very minimal.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.280
We build all
packages from source

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.850
and that helps us keep the
images always up to date

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.760
and with all patches applied.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.190
So we are able to have, for
instance, our Python image.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:23.400
The latest CVE count was
four CVEs against like 579

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.820
for the default Python image
you get from Docker Hub.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:34.350
So it's a significant change and
it's kind of a low-hanging fruit

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:38.760
because you can just change
the base images you use

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:43.200
to build your runtimes and
your GitHub actions also.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.650
And use a smaller image
with a small attack surface

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.150
and less vulnerabilities.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:56.130
The third tip is pull
from trusted sources.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:01.710
So I don't know if you
know that, but 98% or more

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:06.990
of malware is inserted during
build and distribution time.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:10.200
So it bypasses regular
review from maintainers.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.900
You don't see the actual,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.560
exploit the actual malicious
code in the source code

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.970
in the repo because it is
introduced during CI/CD

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:25.470
and injected directly into
artifacts that are produced

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:32.160
at build time and distributed
to repositories everywhere.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.670
So public repos will
download these artifacts

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:39.150
that have been tampered with
and they are different actually

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.790
from what is in the source code.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:46.800
So there is low visibility
about what happens

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.130
at build time usually.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:53.670
So one thing that happens
a lot is a ghost release.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.730
Attackers are exploiting the gap

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:00.630
between the verifiable source
code and the build artifacts

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:04.350
that we consume when we
pull from these sources,

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.080
the public registries.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.230
Public registries
were built mostly

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.800
to facilitate distribution
of dependencies of libraries

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.050
and facilitate collaboration.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.690
And they don't have a lot
of security measures.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.330
Most of the public registries

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.950
from language ecosystems don't
have a lot of security measures

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:29.130
to protect artifacts
from tampering.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:33.450
So it was not a main
concern when we started

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:39.030
to share code this at this
level and at this volume.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:44.040
So now we are faced with
registries that are public.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:48.090
They are also prone
to be exploited.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:53.250
So attackers are shifting
left and they are focusing

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:57.150
on turning developer laptops
and CI/CD pipelines into nodes

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.770
for propagation because that's

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:02.070
where all the credentials
and secrets are.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.190
And you can, with
these credentials,

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:11.010
you can spread laterally and
spread to other projects

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:15.690
and other -- yeah,
other orgs even.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:21.270
So one way you can reduce a lot
of these risks is by pulling

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:26.820
from trusted sources your
language dependencies using

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.500
Chainguard libraries.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.440
So we have libraries
for Python, Java,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.990
and JavaScript ecosystems
at the moment.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:39.060
And the way it works is we make
sure that the build is safe,

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:46.200
the build environment is a safe
environment, tampering proof.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.380
And then we, you can pull
these dependencies directly

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.360
from our repos and you will
be avoiding more than 90%

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:59.610
of the risks involved with build
and distribution time attacks.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:03.270
So we don't execute any pre-
and post-install scripts.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.570
So we don't build
those libraries.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:12.430
You will only get versions
that are safe to install.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:18.570
Another important practice
is pin to digest.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:23.830
Even Copilot pointed out that
you should not use a tag

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.100
in your actions, you
should pin to a digest.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:33.090
A digest is a unique hash
pointing to a specific build

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.150
of a container image
or a GitHub action.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:40.260
So you are not
pulling unknown code.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:45.870
You will always be pulling the
same builds, the same codes

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.060
that you were executing before.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.780
So of course, this will
get stale with time.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.520
You need to update the digest

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.400
when there's new versions
released of the image

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.440
or the GitHub actions.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:02.460
But it gives you time to update.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:05.250
You can use Digestabot.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.940
It's a tool that Chainguard
created and it's free to use,

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.910
open-source, that you can
use to keep your workflows

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.920
and Docker files up to date.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.880
So whenever there is a new
version of a container

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:22.020
or a GitHub action, you
will get pull requests

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.210
with the updated digest.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:29.250
So it's very handy to
keep your digest updated.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:34.080
Yeah, and pinning to a digest is
the safest practice you can use

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:39.120
regarding tagging containers
and GitHub actions.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.280
So this is the action.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:49.770
And, really, you will
define a workflow file,

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:55.260
a YML file with a configuration
and, yeah, then you will be able

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.670
to do these updates
automatically.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:03.210
And, final advice, I would say,

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:07.710
is to ban personal
access tokens.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:11.010
Don't use long-lived tokens

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.720
because they can be
really dangerous.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:17.520
We usually forget
about tokens and a lot

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:22.500
of people give a very long
expiry time for the token

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:26.310
and broad privileges which
can be really dangerous.

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:28.800
That's what we saw
happening with Trivy.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:32.910
They had an org-wide PAT
that was exfiltrated.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:36.390
So attackers had full
control over the whole org.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:41.010
They were able to delete and
create repos, create releases

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:44.340
and do anything they
wanted, basically.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:49.020
So using short-lived tokens is
the best solution to make sure

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:54.210
that you give only a limited
privilege to a workflow run

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.220
and for a limited
amount of time.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.940
So even if the credential
is exfiltrated,

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:02.280
it will expire quickly.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:06.240
So you don't need to be
so concerned about that.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.550
We have an app,
also a GitHub app,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.010
that you can install for free.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.380
Call it Octo-STS.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:18.120
And it implements this strategy
using the same concepts behind

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:20.010
Sigstore and Cosign.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:25.620
So it will give you temporary
credentials and they will expire

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.930
in a short period of time.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.770
So Octo-STS is
actually the identity

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:36.720
that we will pull the request
for you in your repo.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:41.550
And this is the app for
you to find on GitHub.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:49.260
So a TLDR, before we close,
first inspect your repository

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:52.710
for insecure defaults
and other bad practices.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:55.200
You can use Copilots
to help with that.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.750
Ask it to evaluate
your GitHub actions

00:13:57.750 --> 00:13:59.820
and find vulnerabilities.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:02.070
Number two, minimize
attack surface.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:06.120
So you remove a lot of the
software that doesn't need

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.280
to be there in your runtime.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:11.610
And then this means that
you have less entry points

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:16.830
for an attacker to exploit
and less vulnerabilities.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:21.570
So the third one is pull
from trusted sources.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:27.480
We are seeing increase on
NPM issues, incidents.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.780
And it's very important
for us now to think

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:34.920
about where we are pulling our
open-source software from.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:38.610
So pull from trusted sources.

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.700
And the number four
is pin by digest.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:44.790
Don't pin by tag because
tags can be hijacked.

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:49.620
So pin by digest and use
Digestabot to keep them updated.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.970
And number five, ban long-lived
personal access tokens

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:56.160
and use short-lived
tokens instead.

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:59.370
You can use Octo-STS for that.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:06.150
And yes, that was all I had
for this short presentation.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.460
I hope you stay safe
and see you next time.
